Fixed: negative-year padding in `padStart` (src/impl/util.js).

ROOT CAUSE
`toISODate()` (src/datetime.js:1525) builds `"yyyy-MM-dd"` and formats it through `toTechFormat` → `Formatter` with `forceSimple: true`, so the year goes through `Formatter.num(dt.year, 4)` → `padStart(dt.year, 4)` in src/impl/util.js. The old implementation compared `input.toString().length < n`, which counts the minus sign as a digit:
- year -100 → "-100" (len 4, not < 4) → returned as-is → "-100-01-01" (should be "-0100-01-01")
- year -1 → ("0000" + "-1").slice(-4) = "00-1" → "00-1-01-01"
- year -10 → "0-10-01-01"
Years ≤ -1000 and ≥ 0 were unaffected, which is why the existing `[±YYYYY]` test (-118040) passed.

FIX (src/impl/util.js, lines 101-111; only my edit — the pre-existing objToLocalTS hunk in that file was left untouched)
```js
export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
  const isNeg = input < 0;
  let padded;
  if (isNeg) {
    padded = "-" + padStart(-input, n);
  } else if (input.toString().length < n) {
    padded = ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
  } else {
    padded = input.toString();
  }
  return padded;
}
```
The sign is stripped before padding and re-attached, so the digits (not the sign) are padded; the original length guard is kept so long values are never truncated (important for `yyyy`/`kkkk` with 5-6 digit years and for `formatOffset`/`PolyNumberFormatter` callers).

REPRODUCTION / VERIFICATION (scratch script run via `npx babel-node -e`, deleted afterwards)
Before: -100 → "-100-01-01", -10 → "0-10-01-01", -1 → "00-1-01-01".
After: -118040 → "-118040-01-01", -1000 → "-1000-01-01", -100 → "-0100-01-01", -10 → "-0010-01-01", -1 → "-0001-01-01", 0 → "0000-01-01", 1 → "0001-01-01", 999 → "0999-01-01", 10000 → "+10000-01-01". `toISO()` and `toFormat("yyyy-MM-dd")` match `toISODate()`.

